{
  "image_name": "5bfd636bfa1c8d7b3702010f112597ee.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the main ways CRISPR–Cas9 can be delivered into cells and how each route converges on genome editing in the nucleus. It compares three input formats: DNA encoding the system, separate Cas9 mRNA plus guide RNA, and a preassembled Cas9–gRNA ribonucleoprotein. For each format, the image lists representative delivery methods such as viral vectors, electroporation, microinjection, lipid-based nanoparticles, and related particle systems.\n\nAll three approaches lead to the same basic intracellular outcome. DNA delivery first enters the cell and appears to be transcribed to produce guide RNA and Cas9 mRNA, which is then translated into Cas9 protein. In the mRNA/gRNA route, Cas9 is produced directly in the cytoplasm and binds the guide RNA. In the RNP route, the active Cas9–gRNA complex is delivered already assembled, aided by a nuclear localization signal. The resulting complex moves into the nucleus, where the guide RNA base-pairs with the target DNA sequence next to a PAM site. The figure emphasizes that despite different delivery vehicles and molecular forms, the central mechanism is guide-directed Cas9 binding and action on genomic DNA.",
